schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: Documentation for Local Nodes
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: guide
  summary: >-
    A practical guide to community documentation: why it builds institutional
    memory, the four types a node needs (governance, process, historical,
    knowledge-base), tool choices by tier (Google Docs/Drive, Notion/Coda,
    GitHub/GitBook), a priority guide (must-have/should-have/nice-to-have),
    maintenance practices (assign ownership, review schedule), a two-track
    documentation workflow (event-driven and ongoing), best practices (clear,
    concise, current, accessible), and common mistakes to avoid.
  audience: Local-Organizer
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      over-documentation-risk vs the core argument that documentation prevents
      knowledge loss — the article explicitly warns "do not try to document
      everything," in tension with its own case for documenting thoroughly.
    - >-
      accessible-familiar-tools (Google Docs) vs durable-technical-tools
      (GitHub/GitBook) — the article frames this as a real trade-off between
      ease of adoption and long-term platform survival.
  related_concepts:
    - institutional-memory
  related_resources:
    - Notion
    - GitBook
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/documentation-knowledge.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-12f41d6ac647
  source_lineage: documentation-knowledge.md
  notes: >
    # assessed-but-excluded

    - Google Docs/Drive and Coda are named tools with brief descriptions but
      not built into separate resource objects — kept Notion and GitBook (the
      two given the most specific "good for X" framing plus explicit external
      links in the references) as resources, per "small objects, not
      mega-objects" while avoiding a near-complete tool-list dump for
      one-line mentions.
    - No people/community profiles, exact locations, or high-risk triggers
      identified — general documentation-practice guidance, not tied to any
      specific real organization's funding/legal/governance situation; no
      public-use-boundary object created.
